Yes.  There are (many) bee species that are not represented in all years, it is an incredibly zero inflated data set.   From looking at the truncated table output I can see that the subset table includes species not collected in that year.

But why would it do this?  My data is one row= 1 individual specimen. (aka my data is only presence) And if I subset before I make the table, why is it still pulling all species options from the complete data set "all.bee" and not only those contained in the subset "bee2012"?

sitebee12<-table(bee2012$sitetype, bee2012$g_sp)
this table appears to only call for sites by bees in the subset 2012?  (I would understand if I had managed to try to make a table(bee2012$sitetype, all.bee$g_sp), or if I made a table from all.bee, and then pulled out the year data))

* So I guess this brings me back to my original question (this time with more info!)  Is there something inherently wrong in the way I subsetted the data using [], or created a table from the subset?  I dug around stackflow somemore, and have some ideas on how to remove columns whose sum =0.  But man, I'd rather not have to do that every time I subset, when I feel like it shouldn't be adding these zero columns to begin with.

thanks for all the thoughts!
jenny

